userStories.description:
**Goal** — Thread the model tool-call id into foreground Bash registration and expose a non-invasive live output observer in nax-agent.

**Motivation** — A foreground Bash call shows nothing until it ends, so during a long build or test run the user cannot tell progress from a hang (#3022).

**Approach** — `ToolRunContext` (`packages/nax-agent/src/tools/registry.ts:95`). Target: adds `readonly toolCallId?: string`, set by `runTool` from `context?.toolCallId` when present.

Both new members are optional so existing implementers and test fakes keep compiling. The call `runMovableForeground` registers always carries `onOutput`, and carries `callId` when the Bash tool's `ToolRunContext` had one. The replay buffer and ctrl+b replay are unchanged; the listener is an extra observer.

`ToolRunContext` gains `toolCallId` from the call's `ToolCallContext`; the Bash tool passes it into `runBashWithBackground`; the `ForegroundCommand` that `runMovableForeground` registers carries `callId` and `onOutput`, which forwards each chunk the launch reports while the call is in the foreground (the listener is notified before `output.append`'s moved-sink short-circuit returns, so it stops receiving once the call moves). The replay buffer and ctrl+b behaviour are unchanged.

`runMovableForeground` registers `port.foreground(control.call)` and launches with `onOutput: output.append`; on ctrl+b `moveToBackground()` calls `port.adopt({ origin: "moved", ... })` and `output.replay(sink)` appends every retained chunk (up to `FOREGROUND_REPLAY_BYTES`, 1 MiB) to the new sink before forwarding live output. So a moved command's background task receives its earlier output through the ordinary sink `append` path; no tail needs moving.

When an `onOutput` listener throws (nax-agent), the chunk is still retained for ctrl+b replay and still reaches the other listeners. Preserve the launch and result path; an unsubscribed listener receives no subsequent chunks. Omit callId entirely when unknown. Notify observers only while the call is neither moved nor settled; the moved-sink forwarding path must not notify foreground observers.

**Scope** — In: tools/registry.ts, tools/runtime.ts, tools/bash.ts and tools/bash-background.ts; new runtime-to-observer tests. Out: registry-owned tails (US-002–004), card/view rendering (US-005–008), model-facing result changes. Any change to the tool result the model receives for a Bash call. Existing fakes in bash-background-move.test.ts and task-registry-notices.test.ts remain untouched.

**Interface** — Target ForegroundCommand, verbatim:
```ts
export interface ForegroundCommand {
  readonly command: string;
  /** The tool call this command runs for, when the runtime knew it. */
  readonly callId?: string;
  moveToBackground(): void;
  /** Observe output chunks as they arrive, until the call ends or moves; returns the unsubscribe. */
  onOutput?(listener: (stream: BackgroundStream, chunk: Uint8Array) => void): () => void;
}
```
ToolRunContext addition: `readonly toolCallId?: string`.

Test setup: the runtime is `createCodingToolRuntime({ extraTools: [createBashTool({ background: port })], policy })` with a policy that grants Bash, built as after-tool-truncation.test.ts builds one. The fake port records foreground(call); only `_bashToolDeps.runArgv` is stubbed to report chunks through onOutput and wait on a test-controlled gate before resolving.

**Goal** — Add a pure line-aware tail with exact UTF-8 byte accounting and bounded newest-output retention.

**Motivation** — A foreground Bash call shows nothing until it ends, so during a long build or test run the user cannot tell progress from a hang (#3022).

**Approach** — `packages/nax/src/chat/background/shell-tail.ts` (pure, one tail): `createShellTail(limits?: { maxLines: number; maxBytes: number })` returns `{ append(chunk: Uint8Array): void; view(): ShellTailView; bytes(): number; evict(): void }`. A streaming UTF-8 decoder (malformed bytes become U+FFFD); `\n` ends a line, and `\r\n` ends one line even when the `\r` and the `\n` arrive in different chunks; a `\r` followed by anything other than `\n` clears the current partial line so the next text replaces it; blank lines are kept; the last line without a newline is the partial line and counts toward both caps; a line's bytes are its UTF-8 text without the newline. Caps: `SHELL_TAIL_MAX_LINES = 2000` and `SHELL_TAIL_MAX_BYTES = 256 * 1024` (UTF-8 bytes of the retained lines), dropping oldest lines first; a line (partial or complete) longer than the byte cap keeps only its newest bytes, cut on a character boundary. One tail serves both stdout and stderr in arrival order, with one decoder and one partial line. The module also exports `SHELL_TAIL_REFRESH_MS`. Any drop sets `truncated`. `evict()` empties the tail and sets `truncated`.

Constants: `SHELL_TAIL_REFRESH_MS = 100` in `shell-tail.ts`, the most often a consumer redraws for tail changes (the registry notifies per append; consumers coalesce). The ShellTailView type goes in task-types.ts; export the type, createShellTail and all three constants through src/chat/background/index.ts. The module is new; the barrel only gains exports, and existing tests need no rewrites.

**Scope** — In: pure tail module, ShellTailView type/barrel and unit tests. Out: registry table/aggregate budget and event delivery (US-003–004), timers/UI rendering (US-005–008), durable persistence and ANSI colour rendering. Separate stdout and stderr tails: one tail interleaves both streams in arrival order, so a multibyte character or partial line split across the two streams may join or show U+FFFD.

**Interface** — Verbatim type and function contract:
```ts
export interface ShellTailView {
  /** Newest lines, oldest first; the current partial line, if any, is last. */
  readonly lines: readonly string[];
  /** True when older output was dropped from the tail, or the whole tail was evicted. */
  readonly truncated: boolean;
}
```
`createShellTail(limits?: { maxLines: number; maxBytes: number })` returns `{ append(chunk: Uint8Array): void; view(): ShellTailView; bytes(): number; evict(): void }`.

For tests, append means tail.append(bytes) with the UTF-8 encoding of the text shown; split multibyte/invalid-byte cases use raw bytes.

**Goal** — Feed background sink output into registry-owned bounded tails and expose the same registry as ChatHost.shellOutput.

**Motivation** — A background shell opens as a one-shot collapsed listing (`showOutput` in `packages/nax/src/tui/chat/useChatPicker.ts`), so following it means clicking again and again. Background subagents got a live full-screen view in #3018; shells did not (#3023).

**Approach** — `packages/nax/src/chat/background/shell-tails.ts` (one per registry): the table of tails keyed by background task id and by foreground call id, the listener set behind `onShellOutput`, and the total-budget eviction. Exports `createShellTails(options)` and `SHELL_TAIL_TOTAL_MAX_BYTES = 4 * 1024 * 1024`; the registry delegates `taskTail`, `foregroundTail` and `onShellOutput` to it. The registry's `finalize` tells it a task ended through `endTask(id)`. Eviction runs after every append and every `endTask`, and removes finished background tasks' tails, oldest-ended first, until the total is at most the limit; running tasks' and foreground calls' tails are never evicted. The total limit is injectable through `_backgroundTaskDeps.shellTailTotalMaxBytes` so a test need not write 4 MiB.

The registry's `adopt` sink appends also feed the task's tail, `finalize` calls `endTask`, and the registry implements `ChatShellOutput` by delegating to `shell-tails.ts`. `createBackgroundTasks` returns `ChatBackgroundTasks & ChatShellOutput`; `createHostTasks` returns `{ tasks: ChatBackgroundTasks & ChatShellOutput; provider: ToolProvider }`, and `ChatHost.shellOutput` is set to that same tasks registry.

`ChatHost` gains `readonly shellOutput?: ChatShellOutput`, set by the host to the same registry object as `tasks`. `createBackgroundTasks(options)` and `createHostTasks(options)` return `ChatBackgroundTasks & ChatShellOutput` / `{ tasks: ChatBackgroundTasks & ChatShellOutput; ... }`, so chat-host.ts can set shellOutput from the same object it sets tasks from. The barrel packages/nax/src/chat/background/index.ts exports ChatShellOutput, ShellTailView, createShellTail, SHELL_TAIL_MAX_LINES, SHELL_TAIL_MAX_BYTES, SHELL_TAIL_REFRESH_MS and SHELL_TAIL_TOTAL_MAX_BYTES.

`ChatBackgroundTasks` is unchanged, so its test fakes are untouched; the registry class implements both interfaces. Listener errors are logged at warn and the other listeners and the append still run. Unknown ids and agent tasks have no taskTail; ended shell output is ignored. An evicted shell retains an empty view with truncated true. Running/foreground tails can keep the total over its limit when no finished tail can be evicted. Keep the registry thin enough for its source limit and retain existing disk logging, completion notices and task tool behavior.

**Scope** — In: new aggregate tail owner; task-types/index exports; task-registry.ts, host-tasks.ts, chat-host-types.ts and chat-host.ts wiring; sink/listener/budget/host tests. Out: foreground observer registration (US-004), UI consumers (US-005–008), persistent tails and reading additional log-file output in the shell view. Keeping tails across a chat restart: tails are in memory only; the log file stays the durable record.

**Interface** — Verbatim:
```ts
/** Display-only shell output tails; nothing here reaches the model. */
export interface ChatShellOutput {
  /** The tail of the background shell task `id`; undefined for an unknown id or an agent task. */
  taskTail(id: string): ShellTailView | undefined;
  /** The tail of the running foreground call `callId`; undefined once it ended or moved, or when no call carries that id. */
  foregroundTail(callId: string): ShellTailView | undefined;
  /** Told the task id or call id whose tail changed; returns the unsubscribe. */
  onShellOutput(listener: (id: string) => void): () => void;
}
```
New owner exports `createShellTails(options)`, `SHELL_TAIL_TOTAL_MAX_BYTES` and exposes `endTask(id)` to the registry. Foreground lookup may return undefined until US-004 connects subscriptions. Tests build the real registry as existing registry tests do, stubbing log writes through _backgroundTaskDeps; the seam test drives adopt's returned sink, not a direct internal tail append. Verify the host uses this exact same object.
